Use `catch` when a local fallback makes sense:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = parse_number(text) catch then 0
]

The handler covers the complete selected expression, including arithmetic,
receivers, call arguments, nested calls and cast operands:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    total = left * right + offset catch then 0
    amount = load_amount(left * right) catch then 0
]

Authored arithmetic or a valid cast can qualify for a safety catch even when
the operation cannot fail. Two calls in the same expression also qualify:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    chained = source.first().second() catch then fallback
    pair = left() is right() catch then false
]

Calls inside an argument do not add to the enclosing call count:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- INVALID safety catch when both calls are infallible
    value = outer(inner()) catch then fallback
]

The nested call remains protected. If it can fail, its actual error makes the
catch eligible. A safety catch does not claim a failure effect or catch fatal
conditions.

Implicit failure and compatible builtin `Error!` may share a handler. A bound
error has type `Error`; existing typed errors keep their message, code and
identity. A custom error cannot mix with implicit failure. Catch does not
legalise statically invalid numeric work.

See @../errors/#catch-and-recovery (Catch and recovery) for handler
constraints, placement and current support.
